Current fill_span already caches row, starting mapped coordinate, masks and
constant colour bits. Its native path still computes `(start+i)^pixel_xor` for
every byte. The proposed method uses ONLY the same public borrowed row pointer
and masked byte read/store, with the SAME one read/write per destination byte.
No memset/wider access/bit clearing/cache flag/helper/service extension.

Candidate predicate, evaluated from cached values before stores:

    count > 0
    pixel_xor <= 3
    (start & 3) == 0
    (count & 3) == 0
    count <= UINT32_MAX - start

Here start is the SAME existing uint32 `(x_bias+x)` result. For each complete
four-byte group, XOR0..3 permutes exactly that group's four addresses; an aligned
multiple-of-four range therefore maps to precisely the contiguous original
range. The guard excludes wrapping/end-overflow cases conservatively. Valid
original row storage remains required; no invented total-contiguous-frame flag.

For constant C and masks P/M, each distinct byte is updated ONCE by
`(old_byte&P)|(C&M)`. Reordering those independent destinations within the SAME
span preserves final bytes even when P/M overlap. The colour is passed by value
and there is no borrowed texture/source pointer in this helper. Preserve all
span/row/call order; do NOT coalesce spans or reorder rows, so same/shifted/
permuted aliasing BETWEEN rows still observes the original painter sequence.
Existing setup already captures row/masks/mapping before stores; the candidate
must not add mid-span reads of target metadata that could alias the output.

This is a proposed local specialization of the existing supported byte-span
method, not retrospective approval of the original bespoke Canvas-to-row
conversion. Confirm any ABI requirement about observable within-span write order
or concurrent row access before coding; an unsupported requirement stops this
part without a workaround. Pointer lifetime/acquisition/refusal/resource order
and PSRAM byte-access policy stay exactly as established by current APIs.

Expected benefit is removal of repeated XOR/address work across many background/
HUD/viewport fill bytes, not fewer stores or measured speed. The one-instruction
private wall-sample clamp remains lower priority. Count actual calls/eligible
bytes and inspect emitted code before claiming saved work; native cycle shares,
